{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Async analysis for targeted sentiment","context":"## Before you start\n\nBefore you start, make sure that you have:\n\n* **Input and output buckets**—Identify\n the Amazon S3 buckets that you want to use for input and output. The buckets must\n be in the same Region as the API that you are calling.\n* **IAM service role**—You must have an\n IAM service role with permission to access your input and output buckets.\n For more information, see Role-based permissions required for asynchronous operations","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/using-api-targeted-sentiment.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Comprehend Launches Asynchronous Batch Operations | AWS News Blog","context":"### Asynchronous API Operations\n\nThe new batch APIs follow the same asynchronous call structure as Amazon Comprehend’s TopicDetection API. To analyze a collection of documents we first call one of the `Start*` APIs like `StartDominantLanguageDetectionJob`, `StartEntitiesDetectionJob`, `StartKeyPhrasesDetectionJob`, or `StartSentimentDetectionJob`.\n\nEach of these APIs take an InputDataConfig and an OutputDataConfig that specify the incoming data format and location as well as where in S3 the results should be stored. The InputDataConfig specifies whether the input data should be treated as one document per file or one document per line.\n\nAdditionally we can name the job and include a unique request identifier for synchronization purposes. If we don’t supply these the Comprehend service will automatically generate them.\n\nAt the time of this writing the asynchronous operations support individual documents of up to 100KB for entity and key phrase detection, 1MB for language detection, and 5KB for sentiment detection. The total size of all files in the batch must be under 5GB and we cannot submit more than 1 million individual files per batch.\n\nNow that we see what the API is doing let’s take a look at the updated console and start a job!","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/amazon-comprehend-launches-asynchronous-batch-operations/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Outputs for asynchronous analysis jobs","context":"# Outputs for asynchronous analysis jobs\n\nAfter an analysis job completes, it stores the results in the S3 bucket that you specified in the\nrequest","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/outputs-cer-async.html"}]}}

